---
title: How to deploy an HTTPD server with an OIDC provider
date: 2026-08-28
excerpt: A local stack — mkcert, Ory Hydra, and Apache's mod_auth_openidc — to run and test an HTTPS webserver protected by OpenID Connect, entirely on your machine.
tags: [OpenShift, Kubernetes]
---

## Introduction

> This post demonstrates how to run and deploy a local webserver using valid certificates and an OIDC provider.

## Tooling

The technologies used in this project:

- [Docker: Accelerated Container Application Development](https://www.docker.com/)
- [GitHub - FiloSottile/mkcert: A simple zero-config tool to make locally trusted development certificates with any names you'd like](https://github.com/FiloSottile/mkcert)
- [Ory Hydra - Open Source OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect Server](https://www.ory.sh/hydra/)

## Certificate management

To generate certificates locally, `mkcert` is a good solution — it generates a root CA and new certificates following the given instructions.

To prepare the stack:

```bash
mkdir mkcert mkcert/app mkcert/root
touch mkcert.Dockerfile
touch compose-mkcert.yaml
```

### `mkcert.Dockerfile`

Let's create a sample Docker container to work with `mkcert`.

```dockerfile
FROM alpine:3.15.4 AS build

WORKDIR /mkcert

RUN apk --no-cache add curl
RUN curl -JLO "https://dl.filippo.io/mkcert/latest?for=linux/amd64" && \
  chmod +x mkcert-v1.4.4-linux-amd64

FROM alpine:3.15.4

COPY --from=build /mkcert/mkcert-v1.4.4-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/mkcert
WORKDIR /mkcert
CMD ["/usr/local/bin/mkcert"]
```

### `compose-mkcert.yaml`

**Container explanation**

- `mkcert-install` — creates the root CA.
- `mkcert-generate` — generates a certificate for the given DNS names.
- `certificate-permission` — manages certificate permissions and format for use.
  - *We're in dev mode, so we don't care about exact permissions.*

```yaml
services:
  mkcert-install:
    container_name: mkcert_install
    build:
      dockerfile: mkcert.Dockerfile
    command: mkcert -install
    volumes:
      - type: bind
        source: ${PWD}/mkcert/app
        target: /mkcert
      - type: bind
        source: ${PWD}/mkcert/root
        target: /root/.local/share/mkcert

  mkcert-generate:
    container_name: mkcert_generate
    build:
      dockerfile: mkcert.Dockerfile
    command: mkcert -key-file server.key -cert-file server.crt hydra_server example.com "*.example.com" example.test localhost 127.0.0.1 ::1
    volumes:
      - type: bind
        source: ${PWD}/mkcert/app
        target: /mkcert
      - type: bind
        source: ${PWD}/mkcert/root
        target: /root/.local/share/mkcert
    depends_on:
      mkcert-install:
        condition: service_completed_successfully

  certificate-permission:
    container_name: mkcert_permission
    image: alpine:latest
    command:
      - /bin/sh
      - -c
      - |
        chmod 777 /mkcert/server.key
        chmod 777 /mkcert/server.crt
        cp /root/rootCA.pem /root/rootCA.crt
    volumes:
      - type: bind
        source: ${PWD}/mkcert/app
        target: /mkcert
      - type: bind
        source: ${PWD}/mkcert/root
        target: /root/
    depends_on:
      mkcert-generate:
        condition: service_completed_successfully
```

### Testing

```bash
docker compose -f compose-mkcert.yaml up
```

The result should look like:

```text
[+] Running 3/0
